# VFX & environment polish guidance

I am weak on VFX specifically — treat me as needing more explanation here than
elsewhere in the codebase, not less.

When building an effect (kill effect, day/night transition, weather/rain,
ambient particles):

1. **Explain the effect in plain terms first** — what's layered to create it
   (e.g. "a burst ParticleEmitter for the flash + a Trail on the debris +
   a quick PointLight flicker") before writing the script. I need to
   understand the anatomy of the effect, not just receive a script.
2. **Give concrete starting property values**, not just structure — e.g. for
   a ParticleEmitter give actual Rate, Lifetime, Speed, Size/Transparency
   NumberSequence values to start from, since guessing these from scratch is
   where I lose the most time.
3. **Flag the 2-3 properties most worth tweaking** to change the "feel" of an
   effect, so I know what to touch first when it doesn't look right, instead
   of me re-prompting from zero.
4. Bias toward the stylized/saturated look of the benchmark games (Grow a
   Garden, Catch and Tame, Greedy Growers, Steal an Egg) — chunky shapes,
   punchy colors, exaggerated motion — over subtle/realistic VFX, unless told
   otherwise.
5. For lighting/atmosphere (day-night cycles, fog, skybox, PostEffects), call
   out which properties are the "big levers" (Lighting.ClockTime,
   Atmosphere.Density/Color, ColorCorrection) vs. fine-tuning ones, so I can
   prioritize what to adjust first.
6. If an effect requires an image asset (particle texture, decal) that I
   haven't provided, say so explicitly rather than assuming a default Roblox
   texture will look right — the benchmark games rely heavily on custom
   particle textures, not default round-dot particles.
